analysis:
This batch contains documentation only: a new changelog (changelog.d/provider-enabled-filter-wins.md, lines 1-7), invariant 10 added to docs/architecture/model-autopilot.md (head lines 247-252), and an operator-filter paragraph in docs/operations/model-migration.md (head lines 77-90). Together they describe a provider-side tightening. With enabled_models set, coordinator load_model, prefetch_model and desired_models convergence stay inside the operator filter and are refused as model_not_selected. Consented Autopilot downloads and placements keep their own gates. This narrows what a coordinator can make a provider download or serve; it does not widen it. It touches A-004/T-012 (model weights), A-018 (serving availability) and T-027 (weight substitution) only in the restrictive direction. No endpoint, authentication, encryption or attestation behavior changes.

Operational effect: alias migrations converge only providers with an empty filter, so the previous build is retained longer. The model-migration prerequisites already say the coordinator challenge alibi accepts a retired resident build hash, and that a deprecated record must not be retired while providers still hold it. That raises availability risk if operators retire early. The runbook mitigates this by asking operators to count advertising providers first (head line 89). This is an availability and operations concern, not a security regression.

The Swift implementation (operatorFilterAllows, autopilotAllowsModel, handlePrefetchModelRequest, desired_models handling, live filter application in darkbloom switch) is not in this batch. Integration should verify that:
- the code fails closed, refusing rather than falling through to the ordinary path;
- the Autopilot bypass stays limited to consented downloads and placements;
- the empty-filter semantics (serve everything) is unchanged;
- applying the filter live, without a restart, does not leave stale advertisement or serving of deselected models.

No findings, because only documentation was supplied.
